<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prophet's Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">built <a href="/years/622">622</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Al-Masjid al-Nabawi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prophet's Mosque (al-Masjid al-Nabawi) is a mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a>, built by Muhammad in 622 after his arrival in the city. Muslim sources say the land belonged to two orphans, Sahl and Suhayl, and that Muhammad paid for it with money provided by Abu Ayyub al-Ansari. The first building had a roof of palm trunks, clay and palm leaves, and served as a community centre, court and school. Umar and Uthman enlarged it, and the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> carried out a large expansion in 706 or 707. It now includes the graves of Muhammad, Abu Bakr and Umar beneath the Green Dome, and it is the second holiest site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque.jpg" alt="Prophet's Mosque" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bluemangoa2z at Malayalam Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madeena_masjid_nabavi_12122008230.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
